 ### How is the posted pay read?
 
-From the posting itself, or from the careers site's own pay field where there is one. Only base pay counts: a range that includes bonus, commission or equity is shown as such or left out. When a posting gives a range per level (Microsoft's "IC4" and "IC5", NVIDIA's "Level 4" and "Level 5", the company's own level names), the drawer lists each level's range under **Pay by level**, and the headline range runs from the lowest to the highest. Part-time hourly pay is also shown as a full-time year of 2,080 hours, for comparison only.
+From the posting itself, or from the careers site's own pay field where there is one. Only base pay counts: a range that includes bonus, commission or equity is shown as such or left out. When a posting gives a range per level (Microsoft's "IC4" and "IC5", NVIDIA's "Level 4" and "Level 5", the company's own level names), the drawer lists each level's range under **Pay by level**, the card says "· 2 levels", and the headline range runs from the lowest to the highest. When it gives a range for each place (Amazon's pay table, Apple's and Microsoft's ranges for some areas), the drawer lists each place's own range under **Pay by place** and the card says "· N places". A posting that states only a minimum shows it as "from €46K"; an hourly rate that includes the highest shift differential says so ("Incl. top shift pay"), with the stated base beside it when the posting gives one; where a posting states a yearly figure (Austria's minimum, paid in 14 instalments) that figure is used. Part-time hourly pay is also shown as a full-time year of 2,080 hours, for comparison only.
+
+### How are levels read?
+
+Every role gets one of seven levels: Intern, Entry, Mid, Senior, Staff+ (staff, principal and above), Manager, and Director+ (director, head of, vice president and above). Most come from the title's own words ("Senior", "Principal", "Director", "Intern"). Where a company numbers its levels, its own ladder decides: Microsoft IC1–IC2 are Entry, IC3 Mid, IC4 Senior and IC5 and up Staff+, and M1–M5 Manager (M6 and up Director+); NVIDIA Level 1 is Entry, 2 Mid, 3 to 5 Senior and 6 and up Staff+; Google's numerals I–II are Entry, III Mid, IV Senior and V and up Staff+; Amazon's I is Entry, II Mid, III and IV Senior and V and up Staff+; L-levels such as Netflix's are Entry to L3, L4 Mid, L5 Senior and L6 and up Staff+; Meta's "(Technical Leadership)" roles are Staff+. A title with no level word shows **Level not stated**, and comparisons "at Senior level" use only postings that state one.
+
+### How are Google's departments read?
+
+Google's postings name no department, so the site sorts each Google job title into one of 27 job families named after Google's own careers categories (Software Engineering, Technical Program Management, Data Center Operations and so on); a title it can't read is "No department". It reads 97% of Google's titles, and on a hand-checked sample of 100 postings it put 92 in the right family. The Department filter says "Grouped by job title" on a board whose departments are read this way.
 
